WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.965 --> 00:00:08.646
In 1609 Galileo turned his telescope to the heavens.

2
00:00:09.646 --> 00:00:14.188
He looked at Jupiter and was astonished; he saw four moons orbiting the planet.

3
00:00:14.968 --> 00:00:19.289
As the centuries passed, people invented larger and more powerful telescopes.

4
00:00:19.869 --> 00:00:24.891
But these improvements could not satisfy their curiosity about the other planets that orbit our sun.

5
00:00:26.331 --> 00:00:31.657
Instead of improving telescopes on Earth, scientists thought, why not send telescopes to the planets?

6
00:00:32.837 --> 00:00:34.958
In 1977 the U.S.

7
00:00:35.058 --> 00:00:42.782
National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) sent two probes, Voyager 1 and Voyager 2, to explore the mysterious

8
00:00:42.902 --> 00:00:44.102
planets beyond Mars.

9
00:00:45.083 --> 00:00:51.706
These four planets are called gas giants, and they are about 60 to 1,300 times the size of

10
00:00:51.786 --> 00:00:57.188
Earth. Both Voyager 1 and 2 explored the closest two, Jupiter and Saturn.

11
00:00:58.268 --> 00:01:04.650
Voyager 1 also studied Saturn's moon Titan, while Voyager 2 traveled on to study the planets Uranus and

12
00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:13.572
Neptune. From January to April of 1979, Voyager 1 photographed and studied Jupiter and its many moons (scientists

13
00:01:13.612 --> 00:01:14.852
keep discovering new ones).

14
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:22.294
Beautiful pictures were sent to Earth, allowing scientists to research the complicated weather systems that wrap around the

15
00:01:22.334 --> 00:01:30.357
planet. Jupiter's atmosphere to core temperatures can range from -145 to +24,000 degrees Celsius.

16
00:01:31.337 --> 00:01:35.539
Jupiter has lightning and its own auroral phenomenon, just like Earth's northern lights.

17
00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:44.123
When images of one of Jupiter's moons, Io, were sent back to Earth, scientists were astonished.

18
00:01:45.043 --> 00:01:49.325
Moons, it was believed, could not have volcanoes because they were too small and cold.

19
00:01:50.485 --> 00:01:56.888
But Io has volcanoes more violent than any on Earth, with eruptions that send material three hundred kilometers

20
00:01:56.908 --> 00:02:04.058
high. Later, scientists realized that because Jupiter is so big, its gravity pulls the side of the moon

21
00:02:04.158 --> 00:02:06.960
closest to Jupiter more strongly than the opposite side.

22
00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:13.202
As Io rotates, its different parts are constantly stretched and pulled, and this creates heat.

23
00:02:14.003 --> 00:02:18.225
For example, if you take a metal spoon and bend it back and forth, the place where it

24
00:02:18.285 --> 00:02:19.505
bends becomes hot.

25
00:02:21.806 --> 00:02:25.508
Jupiter's gravity is so powerful that it bends the interior of its moons.

26
00:02:26.504 --> 00:02:28.825
Scientists call this process tidal heating.

27
00:02:29.625 --> 00:02:35.327
On Jupiter's moon Io, tidal heating is powerful enough to cause eruptions hundreds of times larger than any

28
00:02:35.387 --> 00:02:36.207
volcano on Earth.

29
00:02:36.887 --> 00:02:41.489
The Voyager probes also discovered that Jupiter's moon Europa has a surface of ice.

30
00:02:43.409 --> 00:02:49.111
If tidal heating warms Europa's interior enough to melt the ice, is it possible that life exists in

31
00:02:49.211 --> 00:02:51.392
vast oceans underneath its icy surface?

32
00:02:52.808 --> 00:02:56.729
Scientists are now designing probes to land on Europa to answer this question.

33
00:02:57.969 --> 00:03:03.831
From Earth, Saturn and its rings appear peaceful and beautiful, but the Voyager probes discovered something different.

34
00:03:05.511 --> 00:03:12.273
Saturn has violent windstorms over 1,400 kilometers per hour, and its rings are not the perfectly smooth objects

35
00:03:12.313 --> 00:03:13.534
we see through telescopes.

36
00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:17.495
Some rings have gaps, like a necklace missing a few pearls.

37
00:03:18.315 --> 00:03:22.196
But strangest of all, the rings have patterns in them that look like braided hair.

38
00:03:23.028 --> 00:03:29.192
Scientists believe that the uneven orbits of Saturn's small moons cause these strange patterns, and the gaps as

39
00:03:29.252 --> 00:03:35.275
well. Voyager 1 also studied Titan, a large moon that orbits Saturn.

40
00:03:36.316 --> 00:03:39.237
Titan is the only moon in the solar system to have an atmosphere.

41
00:03:39.938 --> 00:03:45.161
Voyager 1 found that Titan's atmosphere is made mostly of nitrogen, while the surface is a mixture of

42
00:03:45.421 --> 00:03:46.321
ice and rock.

43
00:03:47.142 --> 00:03:50.864
Before there was life on Earth, its atmosphere was also mostly nitrogen.

44
00:03:52.424 --> 00:03:56.546
Some scientists believe that when we look at Titan, we are seeing what Earth looked like far in

45
00:03:56.566 --> 00:03:58.227
the past.

46
00:03:58.327 --> 00:04:04.310
In 1986 Voyager 2 arrived at Uranus and discovered a shocking mystery that is unexplained to this day.

47
00:04:04.991 --> 00:04:08.112
Most planets, like Earth, spin on their axes vertically.

48
00:04:08.893 --> 00:04:12.995
Their north and south poles point up and down compared to the rest of the solar system.

49
00:04:15.817 --> 00:04:17.017
Uranus is the opposite.

50
00:04:17.838 --> 00:04:23.201
Its axis lines up horizontally, so that one pole faces toward the sun and the other away from

51
00:04:23.241 --> 00:04:23.561
the sun.

52
00:04:24.261 --> 00:04:28.884
This strange fact had long been known, but Voyager 2 discovered a stranger contradiction.

53
00:04:29.985 --> 00:04:35.528
Even though the equator of Uranus receives almost no sunlight, its equator is always as hot as, or

54
00:04:35.588 --> 00:04:38.510
hotter than, the polar region that directly faces the sun.

55
00:04:40.731 --> 00:04:43.373
Voyager 2 arrived at Neptune in 1989.

56
00:04:44.373 --> 00:04:48.956
It discovered six moons orbiting Neptune, as well as a faint ring system around the planet.

57
00:04:49.704 --> 00:04:55.566
Neptune is a beautiful world, a deep blue color with giant white clouds of methane rising and falling,

58
00:04:55.946 --> 00:04:57.927
just as water does in Earth's atmosphere.

59
00:04:58.747 --> 00:05:02.528
After its mission to Neptune, Voyager 2 began to travel out of the solar system.

60
00:05:04.849 --> 00:05:09.830
The success of the Voyager missions encouraged scientists to send more probes to explore our solar system.

61
00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:16.072
The Cassini-Huygens spaceship, after traveling for seven years, is now orbiting and studying Saturn.

62
00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.142
In 2004 NASA landed two rovers on Mars.

63
00:05:20.903 --> 00:05:26.547
These small, six-wheeled, solar-powered vehicles are sending back pictures and doing tests on Martian rocks.

64
00:05:28.668 --> 00:05:36.654
Since the Voyager probes were launched in 1977, NASA, the European Space Agency, and the Japanese Aerospace Exploration

65
00:05:36.734 --> 00:05:42.998
Agency have sent more than 35 new missions to explore the inner and outer planets, the moon, asteroids,

66
00:05:43.258 --> 00:05:44.399
comets, and the sun.

67
00:05:45.676 --> 00:05:48.918
Today both Voyager 1 and 2 are on their way out of the solar system.

68
00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:57.384
Voyager 1 is over 16 billion kilometers from Earth and is the most distant man-made object in the

69
00:05:57.464 --> 00:06:00.967
universe. But the missions of these pioneering probes continue.

70
00:06:01.947 --> 00:06:06.471
They are now studying the complex area of space where our solar system ends and the rest of

71
00:06:06.491 --> 00:06:07.431
the universe begins.

72
00:06:10.574 --> 00:06:15.949
In addition to their scientific missions, Voyager 1 and 2 may play a much more important role in

73
00:06:16.009 --> 00:06:17.691
humanity's exploration of space.

74
00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:22.174
Both probes carry a gold-plated disc containing messages from Earth.

75
00:06:22.894 --> 00:06:27.157
If aliens ever find a Voyager probe, they will be able to hear the natural sounds of our

76
00:06:27.197 --> 00:06:31.160
planet, a selection of music, and greetings in over fifty languages.

77
00:06:33.662 --> 00:06:36.604
But perhaps the most important message is not on the discs at all.

78
00:06:37.224 --> 00:06:42.228
Both spaceships are themselves a statement about one of humanity's best qualities: curiosity.

79
00:06:43.508 --> 00:06:47.791
Like Galileo, many of us on Earth want to see farther and understand what we see.
